WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.769
-Our first guest tonight is
an Emmy-nominated actress

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:05.038
and comedian you know from shows
like "Schmigadoon!"

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:08.274
and "Saturday Night Live,
which airs this Saturday on NBC

00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:11.510
with host Amy Schumer and
musical guest Steve Lacy.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:13.345
Please welcome back to the show
our very good friend

00:00:13.345 --> 00:00:14.847
Cecily Strong, everybody.

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:17.850
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:24.757 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:24.757 --> 00:00:31.664 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:34.733
What a delight
to have you back, Cecily.

00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:36.836
-Great to be back.
-Last time you were here,

00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:39.272
we were talking about your place
in upstate New York.

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:41.140
And you referred to yourself
as a mountain woman

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.776
because of the wildlife
that you ran into up there.

00:00:43.776 --> 00:00:45.745
-Yeah.
-I think, like, woodchucks.

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:47.313
Was that one?
-Mainly woodchucks.

00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:48.681
-Mainly woodchucks.
-Yeah.

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:50.416
-That's why people get --
-Maybe there's wild turkeys.

00:00:50.416 --> 00:00:51.750
-Oh, really?
-But it's woodchucks.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:55.155
-But then you went to Canada
to film a second season

00:00:55.155 --> 00:00:57.757
of "Schmigadoon!",
and this is not like --

00:00:57.757 --> 00:00:59.459
This is not the middle
of the woods,

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:00.726
these photos you sent us.
-No.

00:01:00.726 --> 00:01:02.595
-This seems
like pretty suburban.

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:05.331
-Yeah.
-And those are bears.

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:07.934
-Yes. So that's the house
we stayed in.

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:10.737
We didn't know there
would be bears like that.

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:13.673
They sort of told us
after we lived there.

00:01:13.673 --> 00:01:15.741
And we were in Coquitlam.
-Okay.

00:01:15.741 --> 00:01:17.477
-Which I think --
Then you Google it,

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:19.245
and it was, like,
"Bear Capital" or something.

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:21.915
And there's a lot of pictures
or videos of bears

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:25.317
reaching out for people,
walking trails,

00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:27.887
or living in the suburbs.

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:29.889
-I guess when they told you
it was already based

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:31.891
on the photos a little bit,
it wasn't really breaking news

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:33.225
when they said
there's bears there.

00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:34.460
-Right. It was --

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:35.694
-I mean, they were pretty close
to the old...

00:01:35.694 --> 00:01:37.263
-Yeah, and that one's
got pizza or something.

00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:39.132
It was every trash day.

00:01:39.132 --> 00:01:41.434
And then also --
But the place we were staying

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:43.102
also had a koi pond.

00:01:43.102 --> 00:01:46.172
And I was like, you know,
"I don't know much about bears,

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:47.873
but the one thing I do know

00:01:47.873 --> 00:01:49.675
is they snatch fish
out of water."

00:01:49.675 --> 00:01:51.377
-Yeah.
-So what are we doing

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.746
with the Old Country Buffet
in the backyard?

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:55.514
-Right.
It seems like a very --

00:01:55.514 --> 00:01:57.917
like a place where fish
have no place to run.

00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:00.319
It does not seem like
a very kind life for them.

00:02:00.319 --> 00:02:02.354
You actually --
A friend of yours took a video

00:02:02.354 --> 00:02:03.523
of one of the bears.

00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:04.924
I guess this is --
From this vantage point.

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:06.059
-Yes. Right.
-Let's take a look at

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:07.693
to see one of these bears
in action.

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:11.698
-Bear, get away
from our trash can. Bear!

00:02:11.698 --> 00:02:14.667
-Oh, thank God it's not
getting into ours.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.002
-Now, does it, Cecily --

00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:18.638
-Riveting commentary by Kevin.

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:21.207
-Does it hurt your feelings
when the bear just kind of

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:22.841
walks right past your trash?

00:02:22.841 --> 00:02:24.544
-You know...no.

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:25.945
-Okay.
-No.

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:27.713
-This is the same place.

00:02:27.713 --> 00:02:29.548
You took this photo
of this raccoon.

00:02:29.548 --> 00:02:31.050
-That was Kevin, too.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.819
Kevin would send me these photos
while I was shooting.

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:36.022
Like, a new wildlife thing
was happening.

00:02:36.022 --> 00:02:38.190
This raccoon was sort of like,

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.693
"No, I'm trying -- The handle.
I'm trying to go in."

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:43.996
-This is the expression
of one being like,
"Can you give me a hand?"

00:02:43.996 --> 00:02:46.766
-Yeah. He's about to be like,
"Yes. Right. The door.

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:48.768
I'm trying to go
into your kitchen."

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:50.803
-"I want to eat the garbage
from your kitchen."

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:52.805
-"Yeah, yeah, yeah."

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:54.440
-You did not take
this next photo.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.842
But would you say this has been
a live-changing photo

00:02:56.842 --> 00:02:58.344
that someone sent you?
-Yes.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:00.980
-Before I show it to everybody,
tell me how you came about it.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:06.819
-So, you know, news is tough
to stomach these days.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:09.188
And sometimes I just --

00:03:09.188 --> 00:03:11.991
I really appreciate when a story
shows up in my feed...

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:13.158
-Yeah.
-...that's like,

00:03:13.158 --> 00:03:14.660
"Finally this is for me."

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:20.633
And it was the recent 2022
Comedy Wildlife Photos Awards.

00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:23.435
And so, of course,
I had to look at all of them.

00:03:23.435 --> 00:03:25.104
-Yes, and this is
an actual photo

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:27.340
that was taken in wildlife.
-This is an actual --

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:30.009
-I didn't take it,
but I love it.

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:32.211
-Tell us what...
-I mean...

00:03:32.211 --> 00:03:35.815
-Now, this should --
That is in Florida.

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:36.916
-Okay.

00:03:36.916 --> 00:03:39.251
-So, I mean,
that raccoon looks like --

00:03:39.251 --> 00:03:40.719
I mean, it's like a neighbor.

00:03:40.719 --> 00:03:42.221
Looks like he had
a late night.

00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:44.089
And he's like, "Hey, y'all."

00:03:44.089 --> 00:03:45.858
He's just, like, saying hi

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:48.160
while he's looking
for shrimp tails.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.063
"Hey, y'all!" I don't know.
Just -- I really love him.

00:03:51.063 --> 00:03:52.731
-So it puts you in a good mood.

00:03:52.731 --> 00:03:54.800
When the news is bad,
you just kind of look at this.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.102
You can pull this guy up

00:03:56.102 --> 00:03:57.603
and feel better
how things are going.

00:03:57.603 --> 00:03:58.804
-And I kind of want to, like --

00:03:58.804 --> 00:04:01.341
You know, you have
your phone avatars or --

00:04:01.341 --> 00:04:02.775
Is that what they're called?

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:04.610
-I think so.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.178
-I'm very young and hip.

00:04:06.178 --> 00:04:07.913
Your phone avatars.

00:04:07.913 --> 00:04:10.249
-You mean, like,
your home screen of your phone?

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:13.152
-No, when your name comes up,
your contact.

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:16.255
I just want to change
all my photos to be him.

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.090
-Okay. Gotcha.
-I don't know if it's a he.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.359
-Do you want me to change it
so if you ever call me

00:04:20.359 --> 00:04:24.129
that's what I see?
-Yes. "Hey, Seth! Hey, dude!"

00:04:24.129 --> 00:04:26.232
-It does feel like I'm going
to take out the trash,

00:04:26.232 --> 00:04:27.900
and he's coming home
from a late night.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.136
-He's the neighbor that --
-Yeah, he's on his way home,

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:31.670
and I caught him, like,
sneaking in.

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:35.475
-"Oh, hey, man." Yeah.

00:04:35.475 --> 00:04:37.977
-You went snorkeling
in the Mediterranean.

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:40.012
-I did. Yes.
-Do you like that usually?

00:04:40.012 --> 00:04:44.083
-No. Oh, wait. Which?
The Mediterranean or snorkeling?

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:46.018
Okay. Snorkeling is the one
I've never done.

00:04:46.018 --> 00:04:50.189
I don't normally do
anything super --

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:53.259
anything at all active
or "dangerous."

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:55.762
-Snorkeling is, like,
barely active. Don't you think?

00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:57.663
-But it's in an ocean.
-It is in an ocean.

00:04:57.663 --> 00:04:59.599
I'll give you that.
Famously in an ocean.

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:01.367
-Yeah, yeah, yeah. They say.

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:03.002
-Nobody snorkels in lakes.

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:04.536
I feel like that's not --
like a non-starter.

00:05:04.536 --> 00:05:06.372
-Do they not?
-I feel like they don't.

00:05:06.372 --> 00:05:08.440
Now I'm gonna hear
from our lake snorkelers,

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:09.608
but, yeah.

00:05:09.608 --> 00:05:12.612
-Oh, God.
-That'll be a real can of worms.

00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:15.481
-Yeah.
I want to read those letters.

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:18.216
No, we went --
we were touring the Calanque

00:05:18.216 --> 00:05:19.685
in the Mediterranean.

00:05:19.685 --> 00:05:23.423
-I feel like you're --
you made up a Canadian town.

00:05:23.423 --> 00:05:24.590
-Oh. Coquitlam and Calanque.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:26.158
-Yeah. I think
they're both made up.

00:05:26.158 --> 00:05:27.760
-And I'm gonna do
one more hard "C."

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:30.830
-Okay.
-But I won't tell you when.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.798
-So Calanque.
You were touring the Calanque.

00:05:32.798 --> 00:05:34.167
What is the Calanque?

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.368
-The -- Oh, why would you
ever do this to me?

00:05:36.368 --> 00:05:37.703
-You brought it up!

00:05:37.703 --> 00:05:39.305
-That's as much
as I can tell you.

00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:41.273
-You're touring the Calanque.

00:05:41.273 --> 00:05:43.242
-I'm pronouncing it correctly.
That's as far as I'll get.

00:05:43.242 --> 00:05:44.543
-Okay.

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:48.180
-They're, like,
rock-formation islands

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:49.348
in the Mediterranean.

00:05:49.348 --> 00:05:51.584
-Well, I'm not gonna
follow-up Google.

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:54.954
That's enough for me.
-Please don't. No one do.

00:05:54.954 --> 00:05:57.656
But -- So we did a boat tour,

00:05:57.656 --> 00:06:00.292
and right away --
it was, like, this little guy

00:06:00.292 --> 00:06:03.262
who smoked the whole time
and his name was Nico.

00:06:03.262 --> 00:06:05.164
And he was like,
"But if you want to remember me,

00:06:05.164 --> 00:06:08.834
you can just remember
my nickname is "Nicotine."

00:06:08.834 --> 00:06:11.437
because there were --
there were so many Nicos.

00:06:11.437 --> 00:06:13.905
-You met a bunch of Nicos
over your trip?

00:06:13.905 --> 00:06:17.109
-Well, he said that his company
was a lot of Nicos.

00:06:17.109 --> 00:06:18.577
I guess it's a popular

00:06:18.577 --> 00:06:20.479
Marseilles boatsman,
sailor name.

00:06:20.479 --> 00:06:22.315
But the first thing
he said to me,

00:06:22.315 --> 00:06:24.349
which really threw me --
he was like,

00:06:24.349 --> 00:06:26.119
"And you are the lady

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:28.954
I've just seen on my TV,
a Ghostbuster?"

00:06:28.954 --> 00:06:31.490
And I was like,
"Huh. 'Ghostbusters.'"

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:33.126
I haven't gotten that one
in a while.

00:06:33.126 --> 00:06:36.795
-Yes, Nico. I was in 2015's.

00:06:36.795 --> 00:06:38.430
-That's really funny.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.733
So you were -- You were
in "Ghostbusters" in 2015.

00:06:40.733 --> 00:06:42.535
It's really funny
for someone to be like,

00:06:42.535 --> 00:06:44.270
"I just saw it."
-Right.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:47.139
And in another country,
on the ocean.

00:06:47.139 --> 00:06:49.408
-You think they just --
-And that's someone you trust

00:06:49.408 --> 00:06:51.544
to go snorkeling
for the first time.

00:06:51.544 --> 00:06:55.080
-Yeah. Do you think movies just
get to Calanque a lot later?

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:58.317
-Uh, you know, sure. Sure.

00:06:58.317 --> 00:07:01.053
I'm already in trouble
for not knowing enough.

00:07:01.053 --> 00:07:04.657
-Yeah. We'll just move on.
You did. We mentioned her,

00:07:04.657 --> 00:07:08.261
obviously, earlier tonight,
but you played Kari Lake.

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:09.996
This is kind of, like,
right up your alley.

00:07:09.996 --> 00:07:11.496
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:07:11.496 --> 00:07:14.233
-And, I mean, this is -
there's a certain kind of person

00:07:14.233 --> 00:07:16.402
in the news that you have been
crushing on "SNL"

00:07:16.402 --> 00:07:18.304
for a decade-plus.

00:07:18.304 --> 00:07:20.373
When she shows up,
do you sort of --

00:07:20.373 --> 00:07:22.274
Do you feel a gravitational pull
towards the...

00:07:22.274 --> 00:07:24.143
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.
I mean, I, you know --

00:07:24.143 --> 00:07:26.045
I kind of clock them
as they come.

00:07:26.045 --> 00:07:29.548
And then I think the first thing
that happened

00:07:29.548 --> 00:07:31.483
when I got to work
was Tucker said,

00:07:31.483 --> 00:07:33.619
"We want you --
We want you to be her."

00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:35.521
-This is Bryan Tucker,
the writer.

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:36.889
Bryan Tucker.

00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:38.524
-Not Tucker Carlson,
the Fox News -- Okay.

00:07:38.524 --> 00:07:39.725
-Yes.
-Tucker Carlson

00:07:39.725 --> 00:07:41.560
works at "SNL" now.
-He text me sometimes

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:44.330
just with funny ideas,
and they are hilarious!

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:49.068
Carlson. That's my third "Cuh."
That's my third hard "C."

00:07:49.068 --> 00:07:52.271
He's a -- Yeah.
The hard "C," Tucker.

00:07:52.271 --> 00:07:56.008
No, Bryan Tucker, who is
different than Tucker Carlson.

00:07:56.008 --> 00:07:58.077
And Will Stephen wrote this.

00:07:58.077 --> 00:08:01.714
And it was -- Yeah.
This is my wheelhouse.

00:08:01.714 --> 00:08:04.316
-Yeah.
-I just love someone who is --

00:08:04.316 --> 00:08:07.419
you know, what's
simmering underneath.

00:08:07.419 --> 00:08:11.657
-Yes, very calm, you know,
with like a -- Yeah, exactly.

00:08:11.657 --> 00:08:14.960
The underpinning is very rocky.
Rocky seas.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:16.729
-Oh, yes.
-Yeah.

00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:20.165
-Similar to the Mediterranean.
That was a neat...

00:08:20.165 --> 00:08:21.734
-That was really neat
what you just did.

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:23.236
Do you want to give us
any final words

00:08:23.236 --> 00:08:24.737
about your buddy right here?

00:08:24.737 --> 00:08:28.040
Like, if you were, like,
going on a road trip,

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.142
what would this guy say
as he saw you pulling out?

00:08:30.142 --> 00:08:34.246
-I mean, that guy would,
number one, be like,
"Where you going?"

00:08:34.246 --> 00:08:36.081
He doesn't know that
you're going on a road trip.

00:08:36.081 --> 00:08:37.850
But he'd probably be like,
"Can I come?"

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:39.251
-Yeah.

00:08:39.251 --> 00:08:41.520
-"Or can you grab me
a six-pack?"

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:42.821
He started saying that.

00:08:42.821 --> 00:08:45.157
"You mind grabbing me
a six-pack on the way home?"

00:08:45.157 --> 00:08:47.126
-Maybe he's not -- for a reason
we won't get into --

00:08:47.126 --> 00:08:48.594
he's not allowed
to drive right now.

00:08:48.594 --> 00:08:50.829
-Across state lines.
-He's not allowed to...

00:08:50.829 --> 00:08:55.300
-Yeah, yeah, yeah. He's got
a little raccoon ankle monitor.

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.970
He's out for a cigarette.
-Yeah. He's out for a cigarette.

00:08:57.970 --> 00:08:59.638
He's always like --
And he's always got like --

00:08:59.638 --> 00:09:02.508
He's working, like, a wrench
into the ankle monitor

00:09:02.508 --> 00:09:03.876
trying to like...
-Yes.

00:09:03.876 --> 00:09:06.378
You hear him yelling
about something on TV.

00:09:06.378 --> 00:09:08.848
He's like,
"You see that storm last night?"

00:09:08.848 --> 00:09:12.184
You're like,
"I don't know. I guess."

00:09:12.184 --> 00:09:14.186
He always talks about storms.
-I like this dude.

00:09:14.186 --> 00:09:16.021
Yeah, he's very into storms.
-Yeah.

00:09:16.021 --> 00:09:18.423
-Hey, thank you so much
for sharing him with us.

00:09:18.423 --> 00:09:20.358
I know -- I know
he means a lot to you.

00:09:20.358 --> 00:09:22.861
Thanks so much for being here.
It's always a delight.

00:09:22.861 --> 00:09:24.262
You guys, Cecily Strong.

00:09:24.262 --> 00:09:27.098
"Saturday Night Live" airs
this Saturday on NBC

00:09:27.098 --> 00:09:29.935
with host Amy Schumer
and musical guest Steve Lacy.

00:09:29.935 --> 00:09:32.038
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

